<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Baku Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1747 CE – 1806 CE · capital Baku</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Caspian khanate centred on the walled city of Baku, known for its oil and salt trade and its resistance to the Russian advance.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> in 1747, Mirza Muhammad Khan I, of a local family of governors, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absheron-peninsula">Absheron</a> peninsula an independent khanate within the walls of the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a> city. The town, with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maiden-tower">Maiden Tower</a> and palace, was small but well fortified and had a busy harbour.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The khans controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku's</a> harbour and the oil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> and salt pans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absheron-peninsula">Absheron</a>, whose products were exported across the Caspian to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>; oil revenues were a key source of income. The khanate was too small to stand alone and between 1768 and 1789 depended on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatali-khan-of-quba">Fatali Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/quba-khanate">Quba Khanate</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1806 the Russian commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavel-tsitsianov">Pavel Tsitsianov</a> was killed at the gates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> while accepting its surrender from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huseyngulu-khan">Huseyngulu Khan</a>. Russian troops occupied the city later that year, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> annexed the khanate, laying the ground for Baku's later oil boom.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/baku-khanate.jpg" alt="Baku Khanate" width="320" height="302"><figcaption>Image: Aykhan Zayedzadeh · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Baku_Khanate.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1747 CE – 1806 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Baku</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mirza Muhammad Khan I, Huseyngulu Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Azerbaijani; Persian; Tat</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khanate of Baku</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1776&amp;focus=baku-khanate" aria-label="Baku Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5904 735 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Baku Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6224 991L6298 1144M6224 991L6186 950M6224 991L6487 457"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6298 1144h0M6186 950h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6224 991h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6224 991h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Baku. <a href="/atlas?year=1776&amp;focus=baku-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
